The shipped entry said construction edges being absent from the reachability
BFS made "the constructor and everything downstream of it false dead code for
all eight languages". The mechanism was broken for every emitting language, but
the CONSEQUENCE only lands where the edge's dst is a callable.

Measured after merge, on the instrument the owner suggested: an ephemeral clone
rewound to 9db326c416, run via PYTHONPATH shadowing so the console script loads
the pre-fix cli.py, with BOTH versions fed one fixed 44,842-node survey of
hypergumbo itself. Instrument verified before use — the old process resolves to
the clone and reports the literal {"calls", "dispatches_to", "wraps"}. Result:
byte-identical output. 5201 production functions, 2843 reachable, 2152 dead,
41.4% on both sides.

Zero delta, and the reason is structural: all 11,260 `instantiates` edges in
that graph point at kind=class (8,919) or external_symbol (2,341) and NONE at a
callable, while the 524 class nodes they target have only contains / extends /
decorated_by leaving them — none traversable, correctly, because containing is
not calling. So the walk reaches a class and stops.

The fix remains right and the hole was real (a Ruby fixture recovers three
symbols, two transitively). Its realized value is conditional on the dst
choice, which the entry now says. Filed as INV-rolok, with the sizing stated
honestly: 0 of 2152 dead candidates are `__init__`, so this is a latent recall
hole rather than present false positives.

